hi there steve kaufman here  um i'm leaving uh  for croatia on thursday with my wife  uh  and uh for the last week i've been  studying cerebral croatian we have the  serbian many stories at link  and so i've been at it for a week  and  i'm going to try to speak obviously when  i'm in croatia  i'm going to try to understand  that's why i'm trying to learn as much  of the language as i can in this short  period of time because it'll make my  stay there  more interesting more pleasant  i don't know how much i'll be able to do  so as i'm doing this i i sort of  realized that that because this i'm not  i don't want to be you know a perfect  speaker of cerebral croatian  i just want to communicate and  understand some things  and so i realized that i'm not really  focused in on trying to nail the  case endings or or any of this other  stuff  you know the uh the difficulties of  slavic languages uh aspects of verbs and  verbs of motion i'm aware of these  things  and and i realized that that my  willingness to not be preoccupied with  these points of grammar  is very much going to help me  engage more with the language and i  realize that this doesn't just apply to  sort of a brief fling at  serbo croatian here i think it applies  generally  i remember in learning german when i  stopped trying to ace the declension  tables i actually spoke more got into  the language  the more you get into the language  the more you listen and read and speak  and just engage with the language  without second thoughts or doubts about  the grammar  the better you're going to do  and that doesn't mean i ignore the  grammar  uh you know obviously i want to get a  bit of an overview  although there isn't that much there are  a few things in croatian that seem  different but you come across them  anyway  um for example one of the interesting  things in serbo croatian is that they  have the same pattern as i found in  romanian and greek you know in other  words if i want to  go or you know jelly  or whatever like the uh the the second  verb i want to go the second verb is not  in the infinitive i don't know if it's  in a subjective or what it's in but it's  the same person so  felon milo in greek i want to speak or  melissa fel felona or  vrausavorbesque in romanian it seems to  be a pattern in the balkans that that  when you want to do something or or  wherever you have one word following  another they don't use the infinitive  which is kind of interesting  in russian they don't do that in the  other slavic languages but  a lot of these sort of grammatical  patterns because i'm listening so much  actually i should have checked how many  times i've listened  but uh in one in in essentially a week  because i started last monday  uh i have i did check my stats there  that i have listened i think it is 18  hours  no eight hours sorry eight and a half  hours  of croatian  or serbo croatian i know some 2000 words  i've gone through the many stories i'm  going through them a second time third  time fourth i'm reading and i begin  typically by just going through the  flash cards like if there's 12 yellow  words in the lesson i go through them  knock that  down to  four because i know most of them by now  and then i read through the story again  so i go through the stories i'm on my  second pass here in reading and about 26  because i've been through them once but  i've listened to them many many many  times so when you listen  then without really worrying about the  grammar  certain phrases like i mentioned you  know jealous  or  whatever it might be so that pattern  seems to appear quite often and i notice  it or uh you know often said kokuchi  kokucha is at home  so in russian doma you're not saying  russia damoi is like you're going home  doma is your at home so kadkuche is at  home  ichikuchi  is to go home  so you start to pick up on these phrases  which embody the ending without having  to  worry about  uh  the the declension table  i've become aware that in  serbo-croatian it seems like in the  plural  the instrumental and the data seem to  blend into one  form  and every time i listen i try to notice  these things  and that's the tremendous advantage of  the mini stories is that i know the  stories i'm listening through to try and  focus in on some of the patterns to try  to get those into my brain  as patterns as phrases  rather than as the result of me  reconstructing a sentence based on what  i know about the grammar  now you can only do that so often then  it becomes boring so i've also  downloaded this book  uh  bridge on the drena  i think it's  priya  uh  i can't remember anyway very difficult  like i'm looking up every second word or  more uh but it and i have the audiobook  for that so i'm listening to that and  i'm hoping that in time i'll be able to  understand some of it when i listen to  it but it's also a break from  the stories  it's obviously very difficult and  challenging so that when i go back to  the stories it's fresh again it's easy  it's pleasing and i'm focusing in on the  phrases but i'm not trying to ace the  grammar and so when i am in croatia and  and bosnia to the extent that i'll be  engaged with people and speaking and  trying to understand what they're saying  i'm not going to worry about how my case  has come out uh it was the same when i  was in ukraine speaking russian and  ukrainian i was even on television  apparently i make lots of mistakes it  didn't bother me i was quite capable of  understanding what people were saying  and i was able to express myself  and  i guess i'm beginning to think more and  more first of all as i i said in in  montreal i think we're in this new age  of language learning which should be  more of a  polyglot experience where we can learn  more languages not necessarily one to  perfection although if that's what  someone wants to do  go for it legitimate aspiration no doubt  but i know lots of people who speak  very fluid english  and who are not dramatically correct we  have an employee who is a programmer  he's russian  he speaks very good english he can  explain things he's great in dealing  with our users this is on the wood  product side  getting their  needs and then he converts it into  software but he communicates very very  well but to him articles are optional  you know sometimes he hits the article  sometimes he doesn't it doesn't bother  him it doesn't inhibit him from  communicating  yeah articles don't exist in russian so  no amount of explanation is going to  make it  meaningful you know he can't be thinking  of rules every time he's speaking about  whether there's an article there or not  either he develops that habit or he  doesn't uh the same with the famous  third person singular of the present  tense in english so many people leave  off the s even though they know it's  required there it's because for whatever  reason they haven't developed that habit  so  my advice on grammar  first of all i think people should learn  more than one language i'm increasingly  convinced of that i think it helps us in  all of our language learning  plus it enables us to discover new  languages and if we're going to do that  then we're going to have to accept  less than perfect results  and be less obsessed with achieving  perfection now this is perhaps not such  good advice if you need say english for  your work and your corresponding but  even in english i have talked with  people in europe in japan in english and  they make mistakes regularly and yet  the english works for them wonderfully  they understand and they get their  meaning across so i think we can  gradually get better at grammar the more  we  involve ourselves with the language the  more listening and reading the more  speaking the more engaged we are with  the language gradually we  more and more of the phrasing will  become natural to us without us having  to think about why  and and  in many ways an obsession with the  grammar holds us back inhibits us  we're worried we're uncertain we think  what stephen crashing calls the  effective filter so in language learning  you want as much as possible to get into  your brain you want to engage with the  language as much as possible and you  want to minimize interference  inhibitions frustrations fears so to  that extent i say take it easy on  grammar don't ignore grammar if you are  curious look something up by all means  but don't expect that you're going to  nail it down expect that you will  continue to make mistakes for quite a  while and only gradually improve so to  improve in your language accuracy go  easy on grammar bye for now  you

